  "body": "## Problem\n\nOn 2026-11-04 ~12:49Z, lane code-u's shift claimed a task from queue `repo-health-code-pilot` whose `payload` is `null` — no `kind`, no `issue`, no `repo-task/v1` fields. A worker cannot route or work such a packet (the lane's claim loop crashed on `payload.get(\"kind\")` with `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get'`), and the claim's task id is not recoverable afterwards from the helper's own views:\n\n- `repo-queue list --status claimed` returns only one row (task 8625) despite queue health reporting ~245 claimed.\n- `repo-queue list` returns only ~22 rows total.\n\nSo a payload-less claim is a dead claim until its lease expires: the worker cannot identify it to park/block/complete it, and no list view surfaces it by lane or recency.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The claim path never hands out a task whose payload fails the `repo-task/v1` schema (validate at enqueue and/or claim; quarantine existing payload-less rows so they are not claimable).\n- `repo-queue list` can surface a worker's/lane's live claims (filter by lane/worker and status, with a usable limit), so a claim interrupted by a worker crash is recoverable by id before its lease expires.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Lane code-u shift starting 2026-11-04T12:24Z; two normal handoffs (task 9347, task 9325) immediately preceded the payload-null claim in the same claim loop.\n- The two list outputs above were captured in the same shift, minutes after the claim.\n\nFiled by lane code-u per the file-the-issue rule; the unidentified claim itself will fall back to queued when its 2700s lease expires.\n",
